.he "more (1)                     8/16/26                     more (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
more
- page through a file on a terminal
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
more [ -n ] [ name ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
More reads the named files, or the standard input, and writes them a
screenful at a time to a terminal, pausing after each with --More--.
A space writes the next screenful, return writes one more line, and q
- or the interrupt character - quits.  Anything else writes another
screenful too; this pager has a small vocabulary.
.sp
In a pipeline the keystrokes are read from the terminal on the
standard error, so the text being paged is not eaten.  If the
standard output is not a terminal there is nobody to page for, and
more copies its input straight through, like cat.  With more than one
file a banner is written before each.
.sp
The screen is taken to be twenty-four lines and eighty columns, and a
screenful is one line less.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-n	Take n lines in a screenful rather than the default.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The more that came with the system had options and commands this one
does not.  The flags -c -d -f -l -s and -u are gone, as are the
+linenumber and +/pattern forms, the search and skip commands, the
MORE environment variable and the termcap lookup; the window size is
fixed.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
less (1)
